Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.750 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  across the country from kellogg's cereal  plants in michigan to coal mines in  alabama union workers are on strike we  have to do what we have to do it's just  our lives this is our livelihood they're  demanding higher wages better benefits  and improved working conditions  speaking to workers and labor leaders in  different sectors of the economy what i  really heard was that there's a lot of  frustration among workers and even  distrust of their employers and so  you know these folks feel as though it  was revealed to them during the pandemic  that their employers aren't necessarily  looking out for their best interests and  that they need to advocate for  themselves and this is what  unions are really hoping to capitalize  on  meanwhile many non-union workers are  looking to collective action to improve  their conditions since the covet 19  pandemic began workers from across a  wide range of industries from trucking  to media to museums to higher ed to  bookstores are working to or have  successfully unionized their workplaces  so what's behind this new worker unrest  is it just a passing moment or is this  shift likely to last  before the pandemic labor unrest was on  the rise in 2018 and 2019 work stoppages  ticked upwards with 20 and 25  respectively but pre-pandemic american  labor was not in a position of strength  for decades now private sector union  membership numbers have been in decline  private sector workers were really  beaten down before the pandemic the  unionization numbers were low and they  weren't really striking in great numbers  and they weren't really winning  particularly strong contracts the  economic disruption brought on by the  coronavirus gave workers new leverage  data compiled by cornell university  found that the number of workers on  strike in 2021 increased steadily from  the beginning of the year  so far these actions have produced mixed  results workers at deer just this week  ratified a new six-year contract and  this brings to an end a strike that had  been ongoing for over a month it  involved more than ten thousand workers  at deer it was taking place at deer  where there hadn't been such labor  activity in 35 years and so this just  really speaks to the  i think the ground toll frustration that  we're seeing among american workers and  also the power  that unions feel that they have at this  point in time in a statement the uaw  wrote our members willingness to strike  in order to attain a better standard of  living and a more secure retirement  resulted in a groundbreaking contract  and sets a new standard for workers  in a statement to the wall street  journal dear chief executive john may  said we're giving employees the  opportunity to earn wages and benefits  that are the best in our industries we  have faith that in return our employees  will find new and better ways to improve  our competitiveness  unions built the middle class  unions put power in the hands of workers  despite an endorsement from the  president earlier this year a drive to  unionize an amazon warehouse in alabama  failed when workers overwhelmingly voted  against collective action two closely  watched upcoming unionization drives  include one in an amazon warehouse in  staten island and another at some  starbucks stores in buffalo new york  when employers are fighting unions they  will bombard workers with text messages  or in-app messages they'll also during  working hours require  the employees to listen to  anti-union messages about why they  shouldn't unionize where there's no law  requiring equal time workers interested  in creating a union form committees  dedicated to advancing unionization  oftentimes these committees lobby their  colleagues through personal advocacy but  committees also get their message out  with pro-union literature text messages  events and today social media campaigns  in november the company's ceo howard  schultz traveled to buffalo to make a  personal appeal to starbucks employees  we're not a perfect company  we're not perfect people  i'm not a perfect person  mistakes are made we learn from them and  we try and fix them i think if the  starbucks workers in buffalo are able to  win their union um in spite of the  massive massive anti-union onslaught  that they've received  that will that will be a promising sign  in an open letter to employees on its  website a starbucks executive wrote our  actions in buffalo are not about whether  we are pro-union or anti-union it's  quite simply that we are pro-starbucks  partners  as you know our heritage and culture are  built on the belief that by working  directly together as partners we can  build a different kind of company on  november 18th starbucks employees in  mesa arizona said they would like to  join a union  so is this wave of strikes a tipping  point for american labor and will we see  this unrest continue to pick up momentum  it's too early to tell  i think there are promising signs right  now  employers have the ability to fight off  unions whether organizing drives  or strikes with  greater resources and and more legal  support so it's it's very very difficult  to organize or to win a strike the  current strike wave is not large by  historical standards especially when  compared with the hundreds that occurred  annually in the 1960s and 70s critics of  unions warned that work stoppages and  efforts to influence labor policy could  push up prices for consumers and slow  production we live in a different world  than we did in 1950 there are dozens and  dozens of laws on the books that protect  employees there are safety and health  rules back in the day when the only  place a person had to go for protection  was a union contract or a union  grievance procedure  today an employee can pick up the phone  and call the eeoc or osha or the nlrb  but those in favor of expanded  unionization would dispute that  characterization it's a typical  anti-union talking point to say that  unions were good for a while but we  don't need them anymore but what we see  across the country is that workers that  don't have the protection of a union  contract can be fired  at any time for almost any reason this  is true of employment at will often  contracts state the workers can be fired  for good cause bad cause or no cause at  all in recent years some states like  michigan wisconsin west virginia and  kentucky have passed right-to-work laws  a right-to-work law gives workers the  freedom to choose whether or not to join  a labor union in the workplace these  laws also make it optional for employees  in unionized workplaces to pay for union  dues required for union representation  whether they are in the union or not  opponents of right-to-work laws say they  allow non-union members to benefit from  union representation and union-secured  benefits without sharing in the cost of  negotiating those benefits they argue  these non-due paying workers ultimately  lead to union membership decline  looking ahead both given and de bruges  said they would be paying close  attention to what happens with the  protecting the right to organize act or  pro act earlier this year the house  passed the legislation which supporters  say would make it easier for workers to  unionize opponents of the law argue that  it would undermine workers rights and  stifle business  after the proact was blocked in the  senate democrats added some of its  elements to the build back better act or  the budget reconciliation bill which  they are currently trying to get through  congress

